8046875, -0.01483917236328125, -0.029998779296875, 0.018707275390625, -0.0161895751953125, -0.01387786865234375, 0.0263519287109375, -0.005115509033203125, 0.0064697265625, 0.03076171875, 0.0165557861328125, 0.0248870849609375, 0.002353668212890625, -0.0211334228515625, -0.019805908203125, -0.0159759521484375, -0.0024394989013671875, 0.0305938720703125, 0.0194244384765625, -0.0020885467529296875, -0.01042938232421875, 0.0089111328125, -5.692243576049805e-05, 0.0188751220703125, 0.00499725341796875, -0.01238250732421875, -0.003086090087890625, 0.0308074951171875, -0.003269195556640625, 0.004779815673828125, 0.0025157928466796875, -0.0018100738525390625, -0.00203704833984375, 0.0005884170532226562, 0.00978851318359375, 0.00838470458984375, -0.0196990966796875, 0.0038280487060546875, 0.001171112060546875, -0.017669677734375, 0.0017271041870117188, 0.0243072509765625, -0.0204925537109375, 0.01451873779296875, -0.003070831298828125, 0.005046844482421875, 0.0110321044921875, -0.01212310791015625, 0.00258636474609375, -0.00907135009765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Methods**\n\n*Symbolic Pattern Construction and Tie Encoding*:  \n**Step 1: Algorithmic Procedure**  \nAt each time point, we generate a symbolic ordinal pattern for each oscillator by ranking its instantaneous phase \u03b8i in relation to its spatial neighbors, as defined by the underlying network adjacency. The resulting set of local phases is sorted in ascending order. If the absolute phase difference between any two elements, |\u03b8i\u2212\u03b8j|, is less than the phase tie threshold \u03b4\u03b8, those elements are assigned a tie (denoted \u2018=\u2019) in their rank order, replacing standard ordinal distinctions for the tied elements. For example, a local set [\u03b81, \u03b82, \u03b83] where \u03b81 < \u03b82 \u2248 \u03b83 (|\u03b82\u2013\u03b83| < \u03b4\u03b8) is encoded as the pattern \u20181=2\u2019 (i.e., \u03b81 < \u03b82 = \u03b83, in ordinal positions).  \n**Step 2: Interpretive Rationale**  \nBlocks of consecutive tie symbols within the ordinal pattern explicitly encode clusters of oscillators with near-identical phases\u2014representing contiguous regions or \u201cphase plateaus.\u201d With the inclusion of tie symbols, the alphabet of possible ordinal patterns expands, accounting for strictly ordered (e.g., gradients or traveling waves), tied (plateaus, wave fronts), or mixed local motifs. Illustrative examples are summarized in Supplementary Figure 1A (strictly increasing), 1B (adjacent ties denoting plateaus), and 1C (local clusters), directly mapping topological motifs to symbolic representations. The empirical distribution of these tie-augmented patterns across space or time provides the basis for spatial permutation entropy analysis, computed as the Shannon entropy of the pattern distribution.\n\n*Phase Difference Threshold Selection (\u03b4\u03b8)*:  \n**Procedure**  \nTo select \u03b4\u03b8, we conducted a systematic parameter sweep and validated candidate thresholds through two metrics: (1) within-regime spatial entropy variance and (2) classification accuracy for regime identification. For each \u03b4\u03b8 in the range 0.05\u20130.2 radians, we computed the entropy variance for each dynamical regime and concurrently measured the balanced accuracy of a supervised classifier (using spatial permutation entropy as input) to distinguish regimes in both synthetic and EEG data.  \n**Results**  \nWe found that \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians minimized entropy variance within each regime while maximizing cross-validated regime classification accuracy (see Supplementary Figure 2A, \u201cEntropy Variance vs. \u03b4\u03b8\u201d and 2B, \u201cAccuracy vs. \u03b4\u03b8\u201d). For synthetic data, regime classification peaked at \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 (accuracy >0.90 for chimera detection), declining for \u03b4\u03b8 outside [0.08, 0.13]. In EEG, classification accuracy plateaued within \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.09\u20130.12. Thus, we fixed \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 for all analyses as a robust and empirically justified compromise. As a general recommendation, \u03b4\u03b8 should roughly match the estimated noise-driven fluctuations of phase in the data, with higher-noise settings permitting slightly larger \u03b4\u03b8. Where ground truth is unavailable, we advise plotting entropy variance and classification metrics versus \u03b4\u03b8 to inform selection.\n\n*Neighborhood Definition and Parameterization*:  \nWe structured neighborhood construction as follows:\n\n**(A) Synthetic Data (Rings and Grids):**  \n- *Topology*: For ring and 2D grid networks, each node\u2019s neighborhood included its k immediate spatial neighbors (default k = 2: i\u20131, i+1 for rings; four orthogonal neighbors for grids).\n- *Border Handling*: Nodes at network borders included only existing adjacent nodes, without padding or periodic wrapping, minimizing artificial pattern inflation.\n- *Larger Neighborhoods*: For expanded neighborhoods (k = 3\u20134), next-nearest spatial nodes were included, with edge-node neighborhoods truncated as above.\n\n**(B) EEG Data:**  \n- *Adjacency*: For each electrode, neighbors were defined as direct physical neighbors in the 10-20 EEG system.\n- *Montage Variations and Missing Channels*: Border or missing-node electrodes had neighborhoods limited to available adjacent channels. For nonstandard or irregular cap layouts, adjacency lists were reconstructed from electrode coordinates using a fixed spatial proximity threshold (see Supplementary Table 1 for per-montage and per-subject adjacency).\n\n**(C) Irregular/Complex Networks:**  \n- *Automated Construction*: Used node coordinates to assign neighbors within a fixed spatial radius or degree, validated by visual inspection of network geometry.\n- *Normalization*: For degree-varying or non-uniform graphs, local normalization of ordinal pattern counts was applied to minimize entropy bias (see Discussion: Limitations).\n\n**Experimental Design and Validation**\n\n*Synthetic Oscillator Simulations and Regime Labelling*:  \n- *Simulation Setup*: We simulated N = 100 Kuramoto oscillators (arranged in rings or 10\u00d710 grids) with variable coupling (0.05\u20131.0), intrinsic frequency spread (\u03c3 = 0.02\u20130.2), and noise amplitude (\u03b7 = 0.01\u20130.1) to generate distinct dynamical regimes. For each simulation, transitions between regimes were provoked by systematically adjusting model parameters or applying controlled perturbations.\n- *Operational Regime Labels*:  \n    - **Synchrony**: Global order parameter R > 0.9 for at least 200 consecutive time steps, with spatial phase standard deviation < 0.1.\n    - **Chimera**: 0.5 < R < 0.7, and cluster index (fraction of oscillators in local near-synchrony, defined as >3 contiguous nodes with pairwise |\u03b8i\u2013\u03b8j| < 0.1 radians) exceeding 0.3.\n    - **Traveling Wave**: R \u2248 0.8, mean local phase difference > 0.15 radians, and spatial phase gradient (directionally consistent phase increment across \u226570% of nodes) present.\n  Regime assignments were confirmed in a randomly selected subset (n = 20 per regime) by two independent blinded raters (\u03ba = 0.92 agreement).\n\n- *Dataset*: Each regime comprised 50 independent simulations (10,000 time steps each).\n\n*Regime Transition Detection and Statistical Analysis*:  \n- *Entropy Computation*: Spatial permutation entropy was calculated over non-overlapping 250-step windows (50% overlap).\n- *Cross-Validation and ROC Analysis*: We used stratified 5-fold cross-validation, splitting windows sequentially to avoid time leakage: for each fold, thresholds for distinguishing regimes were identified on training data by maximizing balanced classification accuracy, then applied to unseen test folds.\n- *ROC Generation*: For each window, predicted regime labels (entropy-based) were compared to ground-truth operation labels (as above) to compute the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves.\n- *Statistical Significance*: AUC and 95% bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated using 1,000 bootstraps (e.g., for chimera detection, mean AUC = 0.93 [0.91, 0.95]; for phase coherence-based classification, AUC = 0.67 [0.62, 0.72], empirically significant at p < 0.001 by permutation test). Parameter robustness is summarized in Supplementary Figures 2C (\u201cParameter Sweep Accuracy\") and 4.\n\n**Application to EEG Data**\n\n*Preprocessing and Neighborhood Assignment*:  \nEEG datasets comprised 15 healthy participants, each with 10 eyes-open and 10 eyes-closed recordings (60 s/session; 32 channels). Preprocessing steps included:\n- *Bandpass Filtering*: 8\u201313 Hz (alpha), using a 4th order zero-phase Butterworth filter.\n- *Re-referencing*: To linked ears.\n- *Segmentation*: Data divided into non-overlapping 2-s epochs.\n- *Artifact Rejection*: Epochs were discarded if any channel exceeded amplitude (|\u03bc| > 100 \u03bcV) or kurtosis (z > 6) thresholds, as per standard guidelines (Delorme & Makeig, 2004). Across all sessions, 8.7% (amplitude) and 3.5% (kurtosis) of epochs were rejected on average per subject.\n- *Adjacency Assignment*: Physical adjacency matrices for each electrode were generated per the 10-20 system; for montages with missing or irregular channels, adjacency was reconstructed from spatial coordinates using a 4-cm neighbor radius (see Supplementary Table 1 for details).\n\n*Controls for Volume Conduction and Montage Non-uniformity:*  \nTo assess the impact of nonlocal artifacts, we analyzed phase-scrambled surrogate datasets (preserving amplitude spectra but destroying local phase structure)\u2014these showed no significant entropy inflation (mean difference <0.01, p = 0.41), as shown in Supplementary Figure 5A (\u201cSurrogates\u201d). To further address volume conduction, we implemented simulated spatial cross-talk matrices; observed that spatial permutation entropy demonstrated a lower false synchrony rate compared to global phase metrics (Supplementary Figure 5B). Additional recommended controls include comparing entropy results across different spatial re-referencing schemes and directly with source-localized data.\n\n*Parameter Settings and Analysis*:  \nWe applied \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians and neighborhood sizes k = 3\u20135 (reflecting montage geometry and spatial electrode density). For each subject and condition, spatial permutation entropy was computed across all epochs, and eyes-open/eyes-closed classification was performed using stratified 10-fold cross-validation (preserving session independence). Accuracy and 95% confidence intervals (via 1,000 bootstraps) averaged 0.81 [0.77, 0.86], with sensitivity 0.78 and specificity 0.83; these outperformed global log-variance of synchrony (accuracy = 0.65 [0.61, 0.69"}}